# NetLet configuration.
#
# `pnpm setup:env` writes the .env this is a template for, and generates
# JWT_SECRET. Or copy it by hand: cp .env.example .env
#
# Nothing here throws at boot — the server starts on an empty file — so an unset
# variable disables a feature rather than preventing startup. The tiers below
# say which.
#
# VITE_-prefixed values are inlined into the client bundle at build time and are
# therefore PUBLIC. Never put a secret behind a VITE_ name.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tier 1 — the database. NetLet owns its catalog, so this one variable is the
# whole storefront: products, categories, vendors, stock and the cart all live
# in Postgres. Unset, every listing is empty and nothing can be added to a bag.
#
# It is also where accounts are mirrored, so the tiers below build on it.
#
# Registered accounts live in Supabase Auth (Tier 2b below). Postgres still
# holds one mirror row per shopper, because saved products, delivery area and
# notification preferences are foreign keys to it. Leave that block empty and
# the same endpoints fall back to a local scrypt digest in this same table, so a
# deployment with nothing configured still signs people in.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Postgres connection string.
#
# Supabase: Project Settings -> Database -> Connection string. Which one to
# take depends on how the app is being run, and taking the wrong one fails in
# a way that looks like the database is down:
#
#   Vercel (serverless)  ->  Transaction pooler, port 6543
#   Mac mini / a VPS     ->  Direct connection, port 5432
#
# A serverless function is a new process per request, and enough of them will
# exhaust a direct connection's pool. The pooler exists for exactly that. The
# client sets `prepare: false` unconditionally, which is what the transaction
# pooler requires and costs nothing on a direct connection — so switching
# between the two is only ever a change to this line.
#
# Whichever you take, replace [YOUR-PASSWORD] with the database password. It is
# not the account password, and Supabase only shows it once — reset it under
# Project Settings -> Database if it has been lost.
#
# Provision the schema with:      pnpm db:push   (or paste docs/supabase-bootstrap.sql
#                                                 into the Supabase SQL Editor)
# Add the starting rows with:     pnpm db:seed
# Inspect them afterwards with:   pnpm db:studio
DATABASE_URL=

# Signs session cookies (HS256, via jose). Required before any sign-in works.
# Generate with:  openssl rand -base64 48
# Rotating it invalidates every existing session, which is the intended way to
# sign everyone out.
JWT_SECRET=

# Address granted the admin role when it registers. Optional.
OWNER_EMAIL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tier 2b — Supabase Auth, where registered accounts are stored.
#
# All three are needed together; with any one missing, registration and sign-in
# fall back to the local password digest. Setting them up is documented in
# docs/SUPABASE-AUTH.md.
#
# This is what makes a registration appear under Authentication -> Users in the
# Supabase dashboard. Accounts are also mirrored one row each into public.users,
# which is what every other table's foreign keys point at.
#
# The password is checked on the server, not in the browser, so no Supabase SDK
# ships to the client and the session stays the same httpOnly cookie.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Project Settings -> Data API -> Project URL. Looks like
#   https://abcdefghijklm.supabase.co
#
# The origin and nothing after it. The RESTful endpoint sits beside it on that
# same page and ends in /rest/v1 — copying that one makes every call land at
# /rest/v1/auth/v1/... , which is a 404 from PostgREST rather than an answer
# from the auth service. pnpm preflight refuses it and prints the right value.
SUPABASE_URL=

# Project Settings -> API Keys -> anon / public. A public identifier rather than
# a secret — it is the key a browser would normally hold. It is here rather than
# under a VITE_ name only because the password check runs server-side.
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Project Settings -> API Keys -> service_role. SECRET, and the most dangerous
# value in this file: it bypasses row-level security and can read or change
# anything in the project. Never ship it to the client, never put it in a VITE_
# variable, and rotate it in the dashboard if it is ever exposed.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tier 3 — optional. Each degrades cleanly; nothing else breaks when unset.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Google reCAPTCHA v2 — the "I'm not a robot" box on Create account. Both from
# google.com/recaptcha/admin: choose "Challenge (v2)" -> "I'm not a robot"
# Checkbox, and add NetLet's domain. The site key is public (the browser draws
# the box with it); the secret is how the server asks Google whether the box
# was really ticked. Unset, the box is not drawn. Set one without the other and
# pnpm preflight refuses.
RECAPTCHA_SITE_KEY=
RECAPTCHA_SECRET_KEY=

# The map behind "Drop a pin" on an address. Set either or both, and the
# shopper chooses between them on the picker; set neither and it draws
# OpenStreetMap, which needs no account. docs/MAPS.md has the setup.
#
# Google: a Maps JavaScript API browser key from console.cloud.google.com.
# Public by design — restrict it there to NetLet's own web addresses.
GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=
#
# Apple: either a ready-made MapKit JS token from developer.apple.com (Certificates,
# Identifiers & Profiles -> Keys, or Maps tokens), restricted to NetLet's domain...
APPLE_MAPKIT_TOKEN=
# ...or the three values the server signs half-hour tokens with. The private key
# is the .p8 file's contents; on one line, write its line breaks as \n. These
# three also give tracking Apple's driving time with traffic (3.4.3); a
# ready-made token alone cannot, and the estimate then comes from the distance.
APPLE_MAPKIT_TEAM_ID=
APPLE_MAPKIT_KEY_ID=
APPLE_MAPKIT_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Where a dropped pin's address — area, block, street, house — is read. Unset,
# OpenStreetMap's public Nominatim, which needs no account and asks for light
# use (a request a second, an agent naming the app, the data credited). A shop
# that outgrows it runs its own Nominatim and puts its /reverse address here.
NOMINATIM_URL=

# The display currency's rates (Shared/commerce/currency.ts): read once a day
# from ExchangeRate-API's open endpoint for one dinar — no account, no key,
# nothing about the shopper sent. Unset uses that endpoint; another URL with
# the same shape replaces it; "off" keeps the reference rates in code. Prices
# are always charged in dinars whatever is set here.
EXCHANGE_RATES_URL=

# Canonical origin, used by robots.txt, sitemap.xml and the links inside
# emails. On the live shop it is https://netlet.net (set in Vercel), so every
# sitemap and email names that domain rather than whichever host a request
# happened to arrive on. Unset, both files use the request's own origin, which
# is correct on a laptop.
SITE_URL=

# Origin that serves product imagery, for the image Content-Security-Policy.
# Set it to the Cloudflare R2 / CDN host once product images are uploaded there
# — e.g. https://media.netlet.net. Unset, any https image is allowed, which is
# what lets a vendor paste an image URL by hand and see it appear.
MEDIA_HOST=

# Subscribe & Save (3.1.19). The scheduler presents this to run standing
# orders: Vercel Cron sends it as a bearer token to /api/cron/subscriptions,
# daily at 03:00 UTC (06:00 in Kuwait), per vercel.json. Any long random
# string. Unset, nothing runs on its own and the endpoint answers 404; an
# administrator can still run due orders from /admin/subscriptions.
CRON_SECRET=

# NetLet's own email, through Resend (resend.com): order and delivery updates,
# back-in-stock and price-drop alerts, bag reminders, and a seller's new
# orders. The API key from Resend's dashboard, and the address mail comes from,
# on a domain verified there — e.g. NetLet <hello@netlet.net>. Both or neither.
# Unset, every notice still lands in the inbox on the site and nothing is
# emailed or queued. The same daily cron retries anything that did not go.
RESEND_API_KEY=
NOTIFY_EMAIL_FROM=

# The AI gateway: a Cloudflare Worker that holds the Gemini key and is the
# only thing that talks to Gemini (workers/ai-gateway/README.md is the setup).
# Every model call NetLet makes goes through it — the shop assistant, the
# product and help assistants and product drafting from a browser, and search
# refinement, the "Picked for you" order, photo search, support drafts, the
# review queue's pass and the Arabic name from this server. Its address, as
# `wrangler deploy` printed it, and a secret of 32 or more random characters
# that the Worker holds too — this server signs the short-lived tokens every
# call presents. Either unset, or the secret shorter than 32, and every model
# feature is off: the assistants are absent, search refinement answers
# PRECONDITION_FAILED and the storefront falls back to its own search, the
# recommendation rail keeps its deterministic ranking and drops the "Ordered by
# NetLet AI" badge, and photo search says nothing is looking yet.
# https only, or http://127.0.0.1:8787 for `wrangler dev`.
AI_GATEWAY_URL=
AI_GATEWAY_SECRET=

# Who issues invoices (3.6.6): NetLet's legal name in both languages, its
# commercial registration with the Ministry of Commerce, and its registered
# address. Every invoice is issued whether or not these are set — a missing
# registration is printed as "not recorded" rather than refusing the invoice —
# and each value is copied into the invoice when it is issued, so changing one
# later never rewrites an invoice already sent.
INVOICE_ISSUER_NAME=
INVOICE_ISSUER_NAME_AR=
INVOICE_ISSUER_LICENCE=
INVOICE_ISSUER_ADDRESS=

# NetLet's own support numbers (3.5.1), in international form: 96550001234 or
# +965 5000 1234. WhatsApp opens a chat with that number with the order
# reference written in; the phone is a tap-to-call link. Each is public — it is
# printed on the account page — and each is optional: unset, that channel is
# absent from the Help section rather than a button that goes nowhere. The
# conversations happen in WhatsApp and on the phone, not in NetLet's inbox.
SUPPORT_WHATSAPP=
SUPPORT_PHONE=

# NetLet's support email, the address shoppers can visit, and its social
# profiles, drawn in the footer beside the numbers above. The phone, the email
# and the address default to NetLet's own published ones (+965 5080 8606,
# support@netlet.net, Kuwait City) when unset; set one to replace it, or set it
# to nothing to remove it. A value that is not a real number, address or full
# https link to that platform's own site is left out of the footer rather than
# drawn as a link to nobody. SUPPORT_ADDRESS_AR is the address on the Arabic
# page, and falls back to SUPPORT_ADDRESS. The company name, commercial
# registration and registered address on the footer's last line are the
# INVOICE_ISSUER_* values above.
SUPPORT_EMAIL=
SUPPORT_ADDRESS=
SUPPORT_ADDRESS_AR=
SOCIAL_INSTAGRAM=
SOCIAL_SNAPCHAT=
SOCIAL_TIKTOK=
SOCIAL_X=
SOCIAL_YOUTUBE=
SOCIAL_FACEBOOK=

# NetLet's own account, the one seller payouts are sent from (3.3.21). Needed
# only for the ISO 20022 (pain.001) bank file on /admin/payouts; unset, that
# format is unavailable and the CSV still works. The name falls back to
# INVOICE_ISSUER_NAME. The BIC is optional; without it the file says
# NOTPROVIDED and the bank fills in its own.
PAYOUT_FROM_NAME=
PAYOUT_FROM_IBAN=
PAYOUT_FROM_BIC=

# Umami analytics. Both are inlined into index.html, so both are public. Unset,
# the tag ships with its placeholders unsubstituted and fires one broken request
# per page load — the Pages workflow strips it for that reason.
VITE_ANALYTICS_ENDPOINT=
VITE_ANALYTICS_WEBSITE_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build-time flags (not secrets)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Serve from a sub-path, e.g. /NetLet-web-app/ for a GitHub Pages project site.
# Unset means the domain root.
# VITE_BASE_PATH=

# Build the fixture-backed static demo with no backend: tRPC resolves from
# client/src/lib/demoLink.ts. Leave unset for a real deployment.
# VITE_DEMO_MODE=

# Server port. Defaults to 3000.
# PORT=
